From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] luma: captures keep every pixel, and each shortcut opens its own mode

Capture froze the screen once, painted at the largest monitor scale, and cut
every capture out of that picture. On a laptop panel at 1.25 beside a monitor
at 1.0, anything taken on the monitor at 1.0 was scaled up by a quarter and
resampled: soft, pixelated screenshots, in the file and on the clipboard.
GNOME does the same. The screen is now also frozen at every other monitor
scale, and a capture on one monitor is cut from the picture at that
monitor's own scale, so it is exactly its physical pixels. A selection across
monitors of different scales takes the largest, so no part is scaled down.
A window is still its own buffer, as before.

Ctrl+Shift+1 opens a selection, Ctrl+Shift+2 a window and Ctrl+Shift+3 the
entire screen, every time, whatever the tool was last left on. The mode
buttons are in that order, and each tooltip shows its shortcut. The tool on
its own keeps no shortcut; a person's stale Ctrl+Shift+3 for a selection is
moved once, as the earlier defaults were.

Selection shows its crosshair as soon as it is entered, before the pointer
moves. Escape closes the tool from its own key handler too, not only through
its grab. The Options menu is the Shell's own popover menu: its standard rows
and padding, its natural width, kept inside the work area, and opened below
the bar when there is no room above.

